referenceSolutions.python:
def floorRegisterTotalReceipt(root, k):
    a=root
    out=[]
    from collections import deque
    queue=deque([a] if a else [])
    while queue:
        s=0
        for _ in range(len(queue)):
            node=queue.popleft()
            s+=node.val
            if node.left: queue.append(node.left)
            if node.right: queue.append(node.right)
        out.append(s)
    return sum(out)

referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int floorRegisterTotalReceipt(TreeNode root, int k) {
    TreeNode a=root; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {if(a!=null){Deque<TreeNode>queue=new ArrayDeque<>();queue.add(a);while(!queue.isEmpty()){int count=queue.size(),s=0;while(count-->0){TreeNode node=queue.remove();s+=node.val;if(node.left!=null)queue.add(node.left);if(node.right!=null)queue.add(node.right);}out.add(s);}}}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)answer+=x;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

Produce the sum of node values at each depth of the binary tree, starting with depth 0 at the root. Missing children contribute nothing.

Return the sum of all values in the derived sequence; return 0 for an empty sequence.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(n); space O(n).

